Syntaxes of the “Optional: Try it with the data” activity 

The syntaxes in SPSS, R and Stata of how to:
1.    Create a variable identifying individuals aged 11–15.
2.    Use this to calculate the number of children aged 11–15 in each household.

SPSS
#Use the individual-level data file to identify individuals aged 11–15.
COMPUTE number_child_11_15 = (AGE_CAT = 3).
 
#Group individuals by HHID and create the number of children aged 11–15 in each household.
AGGREGATE
 /OUTFILE=* MODE=ADDVARIABLES
 /BREAK=HHID
 /total_number_child_11_15 = SUM(number_child_11_15).

R
#Load the dplyr package from R library for data manipulation.
library(dplyr) 
 
#Group by HHID to count children, attach the new variable to all individuals in the household, and ungroup data.
Individual_level <- Individual_level %>%
 group_by(HHID) %>%
 mutate(total_number_child_11_15 = sum(AGE_CAT == 3)) %>%
 ungroup()

Stata
# Create the variable number_child_11_15.
gen number_child_11_15 = (AGE_CAT == 3) 
 
# calculate the number of children aged 11–15 in each household.
bysort HHID: egen total_number_child_11_15 = total(number_child_11_15)



The syntaxes in SPSS, R and Stata of how to:
3.    Add this new household-level variable to the household-level dataset.
4.    Merge the household-level dataset with the individual-level dataset using household ID, so that each individual record includes the household-level information.

SPSS
# Sort both files by HHID first.
SORT CASES BY HHID.
DATASET ACTIVATE Household_level.
SORT CASES BY HHID.
 
# Merge household variables into individual file.
MATCH FILES
 /FILE=Individual_level
 /TABLE=Household_level
 /BY HHID.
EXECUTE.

R
#Load the dplyr package from R library for data manipulation.
library(dplyr) 
 
# Merge the two datafiles using the key variable HHID.
merged_file <- Individual_level %>%
 left_join(Household_level, by = "HHID")

Stata
# Load individual data.
use Individual_level, clear 
 
# Merge household data (one household to many individuals).
merge 1:m HHID using Household_level
